    <p>
      On these pages, the same honesty rule applies as everywhere else on the site: figures such as
      MOQ, lead time, capacity, pricing, and certification are confirmed per inquiry, never published
      as fixed marketing claims, and a buyer is directed to verify samples and specification sheets
      against the written brief rather than against a general impression. The
      <a href="/incense-manufacturer/">incense manufacturer</a> page states who we are and what we
      supply in full.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial team and methodology</h2>
    <p>
      LanDeng's content is published under a single collective byline — <strong>"Landeng Tech
      Team"</strong> — rather than a named individual. There is no fabricated PhD, expert title, or
      review board behind any page. The byline is honest about what it is: the company's editorial
      team, working to a defined process that any reader can audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      That process is described in full on our
      <a href="/blog/landeng-editorial-methodology/">editorial methodology</a> page. In short, every
      entity page is built through an eight-step method — establish the Chinese term, verify the
      botanical identity, normalize the English terminology, identify the historical source, separate
      modern scientific evidence, label trade terminology, mark uncertain claims, and review before
      publication. Sources are accepted by subject (botanical, historical, scientific, regulation,
      industry), restricted to an allow-list of authoritative hosts, and labelled with one of six
      evidence tiers, so a reader can tell fact from tradition from editorial view.
    </p>
    <p>
      We separate three kinds of statement and label them consistently: <strong>historical record</strong>
      (what classical texts said, reported as history), <strong>scientific finding</strong> (what modern
      research has or has not established, with source and tier), and <strong>trade terminology</strong>
      (the vocabulary of the incense industry). Traditional use is never presented as modern clinical
      evidence, and an uncertain claim is marked uncertain rather than asserted.
    </p>

    <h2>Why source transparency matters</h2>
    <p>
      Chinese incense knowledge is a mixture of material fact, historical record, and market
      convention. The classical texts — 本草纲目 (the Compendium of Materia Medica) and 香乘 (the
      Xiangpu incense manuals) among them — record traditional uses that are history, not modern
      clinical evidence; the trade has its own grading vocabulary that is convention, not laboratory
      measurement. LanDeng's value is that it keeps these categories apart and labels each one, so a
      reader, a search engine, or an AI system can tell which kind of claim a sentence is making. That
      is what we mean by "research-led": every factual claim carries a source that resolves and
      supports the sentence it sits behind, or is labelled as traditional, editorial, or unverified.
    </p>

    <h2>Our editorial lines</h2>
    <p>These are the lines we hold, stated plainly:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We do not convert "traditionally believed to" into "scientifically proven to".</li>
      <li>We do not make medical claims, and we do not describe incense as a treatment, a sleep aid,
        or an anxiety remedy.</li>
      <li>We do not invent statistics, certifications, production capacity, customer cases, reviews,
        ratings, prices, or authors.</li>
      <li>We do not cite a source merely because it ranks well.</li>
      <li>We do not publish a named author or expert that does not exist.</li>
      <li>We do not publish fixed MOQ, capacity, or certification figures as marketing claims — these
        are confirmed per inquiry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Imagery</h2>
    <p>
      Site imagery — material still-lifes, process illustrations, and hero banners — is illustrative
      editorial imagery generated with AI image tools for visual explanation. These images are not
      photographs of actual products, stock, or facilities, and they do not depict real, verifiable
      samples. Text, data, and classical-source quotations are handled separately: each page cites its
      references in an Evidence &amp; Sources section. This is stated in full on our
      <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>, section 10.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy and tracking</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static site. We collect personal information only when you choose to contact us, and we
      run no analytics, advertising, or tracking cookies — see the <a href="/cookies/">cookie policy</a>
      and the <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> for the item-by-item account of what we
      collect and do not collect.
    </p>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Who writes the articles on LanDeng?</h3>
    <p>
      A single collective byline, "Landeng Tech Team", the editorial team of Zhangjiakou Landeng
      Technology Co., Ltd. There is no named individual author and no fabricated expert behind any
      page; authority rests on source traceability and the eight-step method described on our
      <a href="/blog/landeng-editorial-methodology/">editorial methodology</a> page.
    </p>
    <h3>How can I tell whether a claim is fact or tradition?</h3>
    <p>
      By the labels and the evidence tier. Historical record is labelled history, scientific findings
      are labelled with source and tier, trade vocabulary is labelled as such, and uncertain claims are
      marked uncertain. Each page cites its references in an Evidence &amp; Sources section.
    </p>
    <h3>Is LanDeng a store?</h3>
    <p>
      No. There is no online ordering or checkout. Commercial work — wholesale, OEM, and private
      label — is arranged by email agreement, described on our commercial pages and in the
      <a href="/terms-of-service/">terms of service</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Are the images on the site real product photos?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The imagery is illustrative editorial imagery generated with AI image tools, and it does not
      depict actual products or facilities. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>, section 10.
    </p>

    <h2>How to verify what we say</h2>
    <p>
      Because the site is built on traceability, you can check us page by page. Look for the
      Evidence &amp; Sources section at the end of an entity page; confirm that a traditional claim is
      introduced as history rather than as an effect; note the evidence tier attached to a scientific
      statement; and check that any commercial figure is qualified as "per inquiry" rather than
      asserted as a fixed number. If you find a claim that does not resolve to a source, or a
      quotation you believe is mis-cited, that is a correction — write to us through the
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and we will review it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The structured datasets on data.incenseherbs.com carry the same evidence tiers as the prose, so
      a claim can be checked in both forms. If you want the machine-readable summary of the whole
      site, the <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> file and the sitemap are linked in the site footer.
    </p>

